    <string name="master_data_header_lra">LRA</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_title">What do these numbers mean?</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_peak">Peak — the loudest single moment in the track. 0.0 dB is the ceiling: anything above gets clipped (distorted).</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_clips">Clips — how many times the signal got digitally cut at full scale (flat top, like DAW render stats). 0 is a clean master; above 0 means real clipping. A limiter merely touching 0 dBFS does not count as a clip.</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_lufs_m">LUFS-M (Momentary, max) — the loudest 0.4-second moment found in the whole track. Shows how hard the most intense hit lands (same as “MAX momentary” in DAW render stats — not a live meter).</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_lufs_s">LUFS-S (Short-term, max) — the loudest 3-second passage in the track, e.g. the most intense chorus or drop (same as “MAX short-term” in DAW render stats).</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_lufs_i">LUFS-I (Integrated) — average loudness of the whole track. This is the number streaming platforms look at: Spotify and YouTube normalize to about -14, Apple Music to about -16. Louder masters get turned down.</string>
    <string name="master_data_glossary_lra">LRA — dynamic range in LU: the gap between quiet and loud parts. Low (2-5) = dense, compressed sound; high (8+) = big dynamic swings.</string>
    <string name="master_data_clips_unavailable">* Clips cannot be counted reliably for this format on this device — we show * instead of a false number. All other values (Peak, LUFS, LRA) are measured normally.</string>
